      "title": "SONO: Cheap, profitable and growing",
      "fund": "r/ValueInvesting",
      "date": "July 2026",
      "summary": "The thesis posits that Sonos is undergoing an operational turnaround after resolving its FY2024\u2013FY2025 software and app issues under stabilized leadership. Backed by 9-month FY2026 revenues of $1.2B, $177M in EBITDA (14.8% margin), zero debt, and $209M in cash on a $1.7B market cap, the author argues the stock is heavily mispriced relative to its expanding margins and cash-flow generation.",
      "url": "https://www.reddit.com/r/ValueInvesting/comments/1ectkld/sono_cheap_profitable_and_growing/"
    },
    {
      "title": "Sonos: New Products Chart A New Growth Trajectory",
      "fund": "Seeking Alpha / Gary Alexander",
      "date": "February 2026",
      "summary": "The analysis highlights Sonos as a deeply discounted rebound play following significant corporate cost cuts and aggressive new product cycles. The author emphasizes Sonos' cheap valuation at ~6.2x forward EBITDA, solid unit economics, and recurring expansion within its existing user base as primary catalysts for a material stock re-rating.",
      "url": "https://seekingalpha.com/article/4667362-sonos-new-products-chart-a-new-growth-trajectory"
